  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nDry and unseasonably warm conditions (5-10 degrees above normal) \nusher in the week as surface high pressure sprawls across the lower \nMississippi River Valley with northwesterly flow aloft. Light \ndiurnally-driven winds will go calm overnight, and clear skies will \nallow for efficient radiational cooling. Consequently, widespread \nfog (with some localized dense fog) is likely in west-central MO and \neastern KS, with REFS probabilities for visibility below 2 miles \nbetween 50-70% by 12Z Monday morning. A 500mb low begins to trek \ninto Texas on Monday while 500mb ridging builds across the SE CONUS, \nwhich will strengthen our southerly flow and WAA. Highs Monday \nand Tuesday will range from the mid 60s in northern MO to the \nlow 70s south of the Missouri River. \n\nThe streak of non-November like temperatures comes to an end \nWednesday and Thursday. An upper-level trough will deepen across the \nupper-Midwest and Great-Lakes region Wednesday while a secondary \nwave translates the southern Rockies. An attendant surface-low \ndevelops in the SD/IA region Wednesday, sliding into eastern \nMO/western IL during the day. Given the timing and position of both \nwaves in relation to one another and to the surface low, the \nstrongest forcing and lift will stay east of our CWA, limiting rain \npotential Wednesday and Thursday. The highest POPs (~10%) are \nconfined along and east of the US63 corridor in MO. Accumulations \nwill be light - the LREF 90th percentile for accumulated \nprecipitation is generally around .1\" (mean is around .05\") in \ncentral MO. \n\nStrong NW flow in the wake of the midweek system will bring highs \ndown into the upper 40s to low 50s to finish out the work week. \nPatchy frost will also be possible Saturday morning, with light \nwinds and lows ranging from the upper 20s in northern MO to low 30s \nin central MO. A familiar pattern takes shape next weekend, with \nupper-level low pressure across the Desert SW and surface high \npressure in place over the Mississippi River Valley. Southerly flow \nwill increase as a result, bringing temperatures back into the 60s \nby Sunday.",
